<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William IX, Duke of Aquitaine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1071">1071</a>–<a href="/years/1126">1126</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as William the Troubadour, William VII of Poitou · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnulfids">Ramnulfids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William IX, Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Poitou, is the earliest troubadour whose works survive, composing lyric poetry in Occitan. A powerful and often excommunicated prince, he led an army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> of 1101 and fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> carried his lands to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ix-duke-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="William IX, Duke of Aquitaine" width="170" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_IX_of_Aquitaine_-_BN_MS_fr_12473.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born on 22 October 1071, the son of William VIII of Aquitaine and his third wife, Hildegarde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Because of his father's earlier divorces and his parents' consanguinity, the Church at first regarded him as illegitimate, and his father made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to seek papal approval of the marriage and of the boy's legitimacy. William inherited the duchy at fifteen, in 1086. In 1094 he married Philippa, daughter and heiress of William IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. In 1098 he and Philippa captured Toulouse, for which they were threatened with excommunication. At Philippa's urging he gave land in northern Poitou to Robert of Arbrissel, which became Fontevraud Abbey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>William joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1101, mortgaging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> to finance it. He fought mostly skirmishes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and was often defeated. In September 1101 his army was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i">Kilij Arslan I</a> at Heraclea, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a> he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> with only six companions. Orderic also reports that he later told of the miseries of his captivity in rhythmic verses.</p><p>His relations with the Church were rocky. He was excommunicated in 1114 and again for taking Dangereuse, the wife of his vassal Aimery I de Rochefoucauld, whom he installed in the Maubergeonne tower at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>. Philippa retired to Fontevraud and died there on 28 November 1118. William was readmitted to the Church around 1120 after concessions that may have included taking part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>. Between 1120 and 1123 Aquitanian troops fought beside the Castilians in an attempt to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a>. He died on 10 February 1126.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Eleven of his songs survive, in Occitan, composed under his title as Count of Poitou. They treat sex, love, women, his own prowess and feudal politics. One, traditionally numbered eighth, is of doubtful attribution because of its different style and language, and another exists in two quite different versions. In several poems he honours a woman he calls midons. Pos de chantar m'es pres talenz, possibly written around his first excommunication, muses on mortality and renounces chivalry and pride.</p><p>An anonymous 13th-century vida calls him one of the most courtly men in the world and a great deceiver of women, but it may rest on a literal reading of songs written in the first person. The nickname the Troubadour was applied only in the nineteenth century. His children included William X, his successor, and Raymond, later Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William IX of Aquitaine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Duke William IX</b>, the first known troubadour, wrote poetry in Occitan, and his granddaughter Eleanor of Aquitaine carried the county first to Louis VII of the Kingdom of France and then to Henry II of England.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-poitou">County of Poitou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=1106" aria-label="Poitiers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of William IX, Duke of Aquitaine"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5020 6137h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5020 6137h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=1106">What was here in 1106? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
